\subsection{Normalization $\times$ Gradient Flow Interaction}
\label{sec:mechanism}

The four conditions crossing normalization type (BN/LN) with gradient flow (ON/OFF) form a $2{\times}2$ factorial (Table~\ref{tab:factorial}). The interaction effect is 7.3\,pp: gradient flow's effect on CAR reverses sign depending on normalization type.

\textbf{Crossover, not additive.} With BN, gradient flow improves CAR by $+$5.1\,pp (Full OCC 0.797 vs.\ Detach 0.746). With LN, gradient flow \emph{degrades} CAR by $-$2.2\,pp (Full OCC+LN 0.743 vs.\ Detach+LN 0.765). This crossover rules out an additive decomposition: gradient flow benefits are BatchNorm-mediated, arising from gradient calibration of BN running statistics, not from an intrinsic regularization property of auxiliary gradients. BN is a context-dependent double-edged sword: beneficial when gradients calibrate its statistics ($+$1.7\,pp vs.\ Vanilla), harmful when they do not ($-$3.4\,pp).

\textbf{Full OCC+LN is the worst configuration.} Despite maintaining gradient flow and using a normalization layer that eliminates representational drift (Section~\ref{sec:gradient_dynamics}), Full OCC+LN (0.743) falls 3.7\,pp below Vanilla---consistently across all three seeds (0.766, 0.718, 0.744). This is the strongest evidence for a representation-behavior dissociation: the configuration that best preserves pretrained representations produces the worst safety behavior.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{$2{\times}2$ factorial: normalization type $\times$ gradient flow. The 7.3\,pp interaction effect indicates a crossover: gradient flow benefits are BN-mediated.}
\label{tab:factorial}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
 & Gradient ON & Gradient OFF & $\Delta$(ON$-$OFF) \\
\midrule
BatchNorm    & Full OCC (\textbf{0.797}) & Detach OCC (0.746) & $+$5.1 \\
LayerNorm    & Full OCC+LN (0.743) & Detach+LN (0.765) & $-$2.2 \\
\midrule
$\Delta$(BN$-$LN) & $+$5.4 & $-$1.9 & interaction: 7.3 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

